      "side": 1,
      "message": "That\u0027s a good question. I\u0027ve spend a lot of time considering different approaches - stack, heap, bss etc. IMO the best approach is stack, bacause doesn\u0027t introduce additional synchronization method in critical paths like crashes. Also a separate memory part would need to be used for ISR logging and thread logging. So stack looked like the best, simplest method. \n\nThe main concern is, as you mentioned, stack overflow. I\u0027ve performed static analysis of stack usage for et171 projects and increased stacks for some threads. Even with these changes, usage of RAM is neutral, and the increased stack can be used in a different placed, which wouldn\u0027t be a case with e.g. static buffer in this file. You can see the stacks changes in CL:8607952.",
